    "summary": "Lionel Messi has announced his retirement from international football after 21 years and 208 appearances for Argentina. The announcement was made in an emotional statement following Argentina's recent match.",
    "content": "Lionel Messi has officially ended his 21-year career with the Argentina national team, stepping away after earning 208 caps. The announcement was made in an emotional statement following Argentina's latest match, marking the close of a historic chapter in international football.\n\nMessi, who debuted for Argentina in 2005, went on to become the country's most-capped player and all-time leading scorer. His tenure included numerous major tournaments, highlighted by Argentina's victory at the 2022 FIFA World Cup in Qatar, as well as Copa América titles in 2021 and 2024. Throughout his international career, he also faced periods of criticism and disappointment, including final losses in the 2014 World Cup and multiple Copa América finals before eventually achieving success.\n\nKey figures in Messi's international journey include former coaches such as Lionel Scaloni, who guided the team to its recent triumphs, and teammates like Ángel Di María and Sergio Agüero. His decision to retire follows months of speculation about his future with the national squad, especially after the 2024 Copa América.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, Messi's retirement resonates on multiple levels. Many Somalis have followed his career closely, often gathering in cafés and community centers to watch Argentina matches. His story of perseverance—overcoming early setbacks to reach the pinnacle of the sport—serves as an inspiration, particularly for young Somali athletes who dream of competing on the global stage. Additionally, his departure signals the end of an era that brought together diverse communities through a shared appreciation of his talent.\n\nAs Messi steps away, Argentina will begin the process of transitioning to a new generation of players. The void left by his leadership and skill will be significant, but his legacy—both on and off the pitch—will continue to influence football worldwide. For the Somali diaspora, his career remains a testament to the power of determination and the unifying spirit of sport.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/lionel-messi-oo-208-kulan-kadib-macasalaameeyey-xulka-qaranka-argentina/426270/",
