    "content": "WASHINGTON — President Donald Trump has given the go-ahead for the execution of Nidal Malik Hasan, a former U.S. Army psychiatrist and senior officer who was convicted of carrying out the 2009 mass shooting at Fort Hood in Texas that left 13 people dead.\n\nThe decision followed a recommendation from Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth, according to Pentagon spokesperson Sean Parnell. The execution is to be carried out by a military firing squad. No date or location has been set for the execution, as the Secretary of the Army will determine those details.\n\nHasan was convicted in 2013 and sentenced to death for the attack at the sprawling military base, now known as Fort Cavazos. The case has been in the military appeals system for years, with repeated delays in carrying out the sentence.\n\nThe approval marks a significant step in a long-stalled case. President Trump has previously signaled support for expediting executions of certain federal and military prisoners, and this move aligns with that stance.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the case has drawn attention over the years due to Hasan's name and background, though he is not of Somali origin. The decision may prompt renewed discussions within diaspora communities about how high-profile crimes are reported and perceived, and about the broader U.S. military justice system. It also highlights the ongoing debate over capital punishment in the United States.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/06/trump-oo-ansixiyey-fulinta-xukunka-dilka-ee-nidal-hasan/",
